WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.408
The run you’ll do this week.

2
00:00:01.688 --> 00:00:16.429
The lab for this lesson fine-tunes a small open model to triage support tickets: given a customer message, return a JSON object with a category, a priority, a product, and an order id, with nulls where the ticket gives none.

3
00:00:16.709 --> 00:00:21.701
It’s a stand-in for the studio project’s extraction task, small enough to train in minutes.

4
00:00:21.981 --> 00:00:41.139
The workflow has the same shape whether you use Hugging Face’s libraries on an NVIDIA GPU or MLX-LM on a Mac, and it follows the order in Hugging Face’s LLM course (chat templates, then SFT, then LoRA, then evaluation) and in Maxime Labonne’s fine-tuning notebooks:

5
00:00:41.419 --> 00:00:45.280
Pick a base model and confirm its license allows what you plan.

6
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.315
Format the data with the model’s chat template, and check the rendering.

7
00:00:49.595 --> 00:00:52.027
Score the base model on a frozen test set.

8
00:00:52.307 --> 00:00:53.523
That’s your baseline.

9
00:00:53.803 --> 00:00:58.091
Configure the adapter and the trainer; predict the trainable parameter count.

10
00:00:58.371 --> 00:01:00.931
Train, logging train and validation loss.

11
00:01:01.211 --> 00:01:07.056
Score the tuned model on the same test set, with the same decoding and scorer, and read the failures.

12
00:01:07.336 --> 00:01:09.448
Save the adapter and record the run.

13
00:01:09.728 --> 00:01:14.656
Steps 3 and 6 are what make the result an engineering result rather than a demo.

14
00:01:14.936 --> 00:01:21.229
Everything else is plumbing, and this lesson shows the plumbing as the current docs describe it (TRL 1.

15
00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:22.469
17.

16
00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:24.029
1 and PEFT 0.

17
00:01:24.309 --> 00:01:25.205
21.

18
00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:27.021
2 at the time of writing).
